entry linear-accelerator
term: Linear Accelerator (LINAC)
difficulty: advanced
image: ../../assets/images/terms/linac.webp
related_terms: cyclotron
related_terms: x-ray
related_terms: gamma-ray
related_terms: john-cockcroft
quick_facts: Accelerates particles in a straight line
quick_facts: The workhorse machine of modern cancer radiotherapy
quick_facts: Medical units: electrons at 6–25 MeV → therapeutic X-rays
quick_facts: Also serves as the injector front-end for big research machines
quick_facts: Microwave cavities give particles repeated energy kicks

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Radiotherapy","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/radiotherapy"}

definition:
    A particle accelerator that pushes charged particles down a straight tube using oscillating electromagnetic fields, adding a kick each segment. Medical LINACs accelerate electrons to millions of electron-volts and slam them into metal targets to produce precisely shaped X-ray beams for cancer radiotherapy — the machine behind most modern radiation oncology. In research, giant LINACs serve as injectors for synchrotrons and as precision probes of nuclear structure.

debunking_snippet:
    “Radiotherapy machines are experimental.” Modern LINACs deliver sub-millimeter-guided beams daily in thousands of hospitals — they're among the most precise routinely-used devices in medicine.
